ith an Ace, King, and Queen kicker.\nYou have four cards to a Royal Flush/Straight Flush (Ac, Kc, Qc ... missing 10c/Jc) and four cards to a generic straight (A, K, Q, J ... missing 10), but you do not have a made straight or flush.\nThe board is very "wet" (dangerous) with three clubs and three broadway cards (A, K, J).\nThe opponent has checked every street (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, River). This extreme passivity suggests they likely missed the board, have a weak pair, or are slow-playing a monster (though less likely to check the river with a monster).\nYour pair of Jacks has decent showdown value in a pot where no aggression has been shown.\n\nReason:\nBetting here is known as a "thin value bet" or a bluff. However, if you bet:\n1.  Better hands (Ax, Kx, any Flush, Straight with a 10) will call or raise you.\n2.  Worse hands (9x, 2x, air) will likely fold because the board is scary (A, K, flush potential).\nTherefore, betting does not gain value from worse hands and only loses money to better hands. Checking back guarantees you see the showdown for free. Since you likely have the best hand against a random weak range but cannot get value from it, checking is the optimal Expected Value (EV) play.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
